artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let G = (V, E) be a connected undirected graph with n = |V| vertices and m = |E| edges, where each edge e ∈ E carries a real weight w(e). A minimum spanning tree (MST) of G is a spanning tree T ⊆ E minimizing the total weight ∑_{e ∈ T} w(e). In the comparison-based model of computation (where edge weights are accessed only via pairwise comparisons and standard pointer/RAM operations are allowed in O(1) time), it is known that:

(i) there exists a randomized (Las Vegas) algorithm computing an MST in O(m + n) expected time;
(ii) there exists a deterministic algorithm running in O(m · α(m, n)) time, where α is the inverse Ackermann function;
(iii) there exists a deterministic algorithm whose running time matches the optimal decision-tree complexity for the MST problem, though no explicit bound better than O(m · α(m, n)) is known for it.

Question: Does there exist a deterministic algorithm that, given any such connected weighted graph G, computes a minimum spanning tree of G in O(m + n) worst-case time? Equivalently, is randomization necessary to achieve linear-time computation of minimum spanning trees?

